SNAPSHOT TESTS
==============

Run the compiler on a project and keep its output as well as some intermediate state in files,
so that a change to the compiler shows up as a diff.
Think of them as a _contrast agent_ that alerts us to changes.


DESCRIPTION
-----------

A project's snapshots live beside it, under its urt directory: next to
`hello.urp` is `hello.urt`, and the snapshots are `hello.urt/snapshot`.  Each
case there is a run of the compiler, with the flags it was given and what it
wrote:

  hello.ur
  hello.urp
  hello.urt/
  `-- snapshot/
      `-- 1/                      a case; named, by a number unless told otherwise
          |-- args                extra flags, if any; the project is implied
          `-- expected/
              |-- exitcode        the compiler's exit status
              |-- stdout.txt      what the compiler wrote to stdout
              |-- stderr.txt      ... and to stderr
              |-- settings.txt    -saveSettings   the parsed and merged job
              |-- parsetree.ur    -saveParsetree  the program as parsed
              |-- typecheck.urs   -saveTypecheck  the signatures inferred
              |-- schema.sql      -sql            the database schema
              |-- client.js       -saveJs         the client-side script
              |-- endpoints.json  -endpoints      the URLs the app serves
              `-- server.c        -saveC          the generated C

One project can have several cases (the same program under three database
backends, say), each with its own args and the artifacts it tracks:

  shop.urt/
  `-- snapshot/
      |-- mysql/
      |   |-- args                -dbms mysql
      |   `-- expected/
      |       `-- schema.sql
      |-- postgres/
      |   `-- ...
      `-- sqlite/
          `-- ...

A case can go on from the compiler to the application it compiles: under
request-response/ are runs, each the application started afresh and sent
requests, one after the other in the order of their names:

  shop.urt/
  `-- snapshot/
      `-- sqlite/
          |-- args                        -dbms sqlite
          |-- expected/
          |-- app.exe                     the application as last built; not for git
          `-- request-response/
              `-- checkout/               a run; named like a case
                  |-- args                extra flags for the application, if any
                  |-- env                 its environment, NAME=value a line, if any
                  |-- secret              the key it signs with: 16 bytes or more
                  |-- db.sql              the database it starts from, if it uses one
                  |-- interaction/
                  |   |-- 01-add.request  what to send
                  |   |-- 01-add.response what came back, as `curl -i` prints it
                  |   |-- 01-add.dml.sql  what that changed in the database, if anything
                  |   |-- 01-add.stdout   what the server printed meanwhile, if anything
                  |   |-- 01-add.stderr
                  |   |-- 02-pay.request
                  |   |-- 02-pay.response
                  |   |-- 03-mail.wait    a line to wait for the server to print
                  |   |-- 03-mail.stdout  what it printed meanwhile, as for a request
                  |   |-- 04-ship.request
                  |   `-- 04-ship.error   why the request could not be sent, while it cannot
                  `-- out/
                      |-- startup.dml.sql what the application did before the first
                      |-- startup.stdout  request (task initialize), if anything
                      |-- startup.stderr
                      |-- shutdown.stdout what it printed after the last, if anything
                      |-- shutdown.stderr
                      `-- shutdown.db.sql the database it left behind

A request is a method, a path, and then fields (-d), cookies (-b) and headers
(-H) in curl's spelling, quoted as for a shell; nothing else is taken:

  POST /Shop/add
      -d 'name=kettle'
      -d 'price=12'
      -b 'visitor=somebody'
      -H 'Accept: text/html'

It is sent as it stands, so it says all of it: a path has what it cannot carry
(a blank, a #, a control character) percent-encoded, a header or cookie holds
no line break, and nothing is read from a file (-d @file, -b file, -H @file).

What the server prints for a request goes beside its response, and only if
there is any: its own lines about connections and requests are dropped, the
program's debug output and the runtime's errors are kept.  A request that
cannot be sent at all (the server died, or hangs) stops the run: NAME.error
says why, the records of the requests after it go, and the run fails until
it gets past the request again.

A step can wait instead of send, for what the application does on its own
(a task periodic): NAME.wait holds one line,

  until still running

and the run goes on once the server has printed a line holding the text after
'until', the blanks around it aside, on stdout or stderr, since what the step
before took.  A line counts as the recording has it: one of the server's own
lines, which are dropped, does not.  What the server printed on that stream up
to the end of the line, what it printed on the other, and what the database saw
meanwhile go under NAME, as for a request (NAME.stdout, NAME.stderr, NAME.dml.sql);
there is no response.  What comes after the line on its stream is left to the
next step; on the other stream and in the database it may land in either, so the
line waited for is best printed last.  A wait lasts as long as it takes, not a
set time, so that what is recorded does not depend on the machine; if the line
has not come within 30 seconds, or the server is gone, the run fails.
A wait that comes first takes what the server printed while starting as well,
which then is not under out/: a task starts with the server, and whether its
first lines come before the server says it is listening or after is a matter
of chance.

Runs share nothing.  Each has its own database -- SQLite, so the case's args
need -dbms sqlite -- loaded from db.sql, which holds the schema and any rows
to start with; within a run, the requests build on each other.  So that the
same requests get the same answers, the application's clock is pinned (the
first request is served at 2000-01-01 00:00:00 UTC, each further one a second
later, and the database agrees), as are its random numbers and its time zone,
UTC; its heap starts at 256 MiB, so that no handler is run a second time
for the heap to grow; every request is for the host 'localhost', from the
user agent 'urt'.

A run's env adds to the application's environment, NAME=value a line, the
value taken as written, though it holds no control character but a tab;
blank lines and lines starting with # are skipped.
What urt sets itself for the above cannot be set there: URWEB_SIGFILE_PATH,
URWEB_SQLITE_DB_PATH, URWEB_DML_LOG, URWEB_REPRODUCIBLE_EPOCH,
URWEB_REPRODUCIBLE_PRNG_SEED, URWEB_MIN_HEAP and TZ.

A case with runs is compiled all the way, so its args cannot hold -stop or
-tc, and it fails if that does not give an application.  The compiler builds
it where the project has it built, and from there it is moved into the case
as app.exe, where it stays, to be started by hand if need be.
A run's args cannot hold -t: its requests are sent one after the other, and
with more threads than one it is a matter of chance which of them answers.
Nor --, after which the server would ignore the flags urt adds (the address,
the port range, -d3).  Runs need curl and sqlite3 (3.37 or later) on the PATH.

A case can also have runs in a browser, under browser/: the application
started afresh as above, once per browser engine -- Chromium, Firefox and
WebKit, all three unless the run's 'engines' file names fewer -- and the
engine sent through it, step by step, in one tab or several:

  shop.urt/
  `-- snapshot/
      `-- sqlite/
          `-- browser/
              `-- checkout/             a run; args, env, secret, db.sql and out/ as above
                  |-- engines             the engines to drive, one per line, if not all
                  |-- bob.context         how a user's browser is set up, if not the usual way
                  |-- screenshots/        every page as drawn, with --screenshot; not for git
                  |   `-- 01-open.user.1.chromium.png
                  `-- interaction/
                      |-- 01-open.step      what to do, and in which tab (user.1)
                      |-- 01-open.user.1.page.html
                      |                     the tab's page afterwards: its path, then the DOM
                      |                     as the browser has it, scripts and handlers left out
                      |-- 01-open.user.1.requests
                      |                     what the tab fetched, with the status of each
                      |-- 01-open.user.1.dialogs
                      |                     the alerts, confirms and prompts, with their answers
                      |-- 01-open.user.1.console
                      |                     what the tab logged, and its script's errors
                      |-- 01-open.dml.sql   what the step changed in the database, if anything
                      |-- 01-open.stdout    what the server printed meanwhile, if anything
                      |-- 01-open.stderr
                      |-- 02-add.step
                      |-- 02-add.user.1.page.html
                      |                     what the engines agree on, shared
                      |-- 02-add.user.1.page.firefox.html
                      |                     what one of them records differently, its own
                      |-- 03-pay.step
                      `-- 03-pay.error      why the step could not be done, while it cannot

The engines agree on nearly everything, and a record they agree on is one
file; one that an engine records differently is that engine's own, beside
it, with the engine's name after the record's kind, and what that engine is
compared against.  Recording goes by the majority: the engines agreeing in
the greatest number write the shared file, the others their own, an own file
whose engine has come to agree goes, and a tie goes to the engine first in
the order chromium, firefox, webkit.

A step holds actions, one per line, split as for a shell, each for a tab,
USER.TAB: -- a user is a browser of their own, cookies and all, a tab a page
in it, a client to the application; user.1 is the usual one, bob.1 another
user, user.2 a second tab of the first.  A tab comes into being with its
first action, an open, and goes with close; after every step, every tab that
is open is recorded.

  user.1: open /Shop/main
  user.1: fill 'input[name=name]' kettle
  user.1: click 'text=Add'
  bob.1: open /Shop/main
  bob.1: wait 'text=1 item'

`urt-browse --help` lists the actions and their locators, which are
Playwright's, and the options a USER.context can set up a user's browser
with (the window, the colour scheme, the locale, ...), each on a line of its
own, OPTION VALUE; every user's browser is 1024x768, en-US, UTC and the user
agent 'urt' unless theirs says otherwise.  With --screenshot, every tab is
shot after every step as well, the whole page as the engine draws it, under
the run's screenshots/ as NAME.USER.TAB.ENGINE.png, the directory emptied
first: pictures to look at when a record wants explaining, which git ignores
and nothing compares, since no two engines draw alike; the records are the
same with and without.
The pages' clock stands still at the run's start, so that
nothing happens between steps that a step did not ask for: `tick 2` lets two
seconds pass for every page, firing what was due (sleep, timeouts), one
user's browser after another, each settled before the next.  The pages'
requests reach the server in the order the engine makes them, and move its
clock on as curl's would; no engine caches or fetches a favicon, so that each
sends the same requests.  A request for any other server is refused by the
driver and recorded as such: a run drives this application, and the network
is not its business; what the pages need, the application serves (the
'file' directive).  One engine difference is left as it is: back to a
page that answered a POST sends the POST again in Chromium and WebKit, and is
refused by Firefox, where the step cannot be done and says so; a run meant for
every engine opens the page instead.
A step that cannot be done -- no such element, two of them, a wait that
times out -- stops the run, as a request that cannot be sent does: the step's
NAME.error holds the engine's complaint, beside its records of the pages as
the engine found them, the records of the steps after it go, and the run fails
until it gets past the step again.  An engine that stops where the others go
on has an own NAME.ENGINE.error, and own files, empty, for what it did not
record after that.
Browser runs need urt-browse beside urt, python3 with Playwright, and the
engines where PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH says; the compiler's nix-shell and its
package with urt have all of it.

Nothing else may be under snapshot/, bar a README.md beside the cases or in
one, or beside the runs or in one: a file this program would not read is
refused rather than left to rot.
The urt directory itself may hold a README.md, and a 'project' file naming
the project when the directory's name does not; other tools keep their own
directories there.


USAGE
-----

  urt snapshot [PATH...]                     update the snapshots below the given projects
  urt snapshot --whine [PATH...]             fail on a diff (you want this in CI)
  urt snapshot --screenshot [PATH...]        also shoot every page of the browser runs
  urt snapshot --list [PATH...]              list the cases matching the given path(s)
  urt snapshot --create [PROJECT] [CASE...]  create cases for the given project
  urt snapshot --create-run [PATH] [RUN...]  create runs for the one case at the given path
  urt snapshot --create-browser-run [PATH] [RUN...]  the same, for runs in a browser
  urt snapshot --edit [PATH] [ARTIFACT...]   edit the tracked set of a case

PATH is a project (demo/alert[.urp]), its urt directory (demo/alert.urt), its
snapshots (demo/alert.urt/snapshot), one case (demo/alert.urt/snapshot/1) or a
directory to search for snapshots.  It defaults to the current directory.

With '--create', cases are set up for a project, named as given or by the
next number: an empty 'args' file and an 'expected' directory of empty
artifacts each.  On a terminal the artifacts to track are ticked in a
checklist and a run is offered to fill them in; otherwise all of them are
made, to be pruned by hand and filled in by a plain run.  With no PROJECT it
takes the only .urp in the current directory.

With '--create-run', runs are set up for a case, named as given or by the
next number: an empty 'args', a 'secret', the schema as the compiler gives it
for the case as 'db.sql' if the program has tables, and a first request to
replace.  '--create-browser-run' does the same under browser/, with a first
step, 'user.1: open /', to replace.  A plain run fills in the rest.

With '--edit', the artifacts a case tracks are ticked in a checklist.  PATH
names the case, or a project or directory holding several, to choose from;
the current directory otherwise.  A newly tracked artifact starts empty, and
a run is offered to fill it in.  Named outright, the ARTIFACTs become the
tracked set without asking, for scripts.

The compiler is the one URWEB names, run with the flags in URWEB_FLAGS if
that is set, or else the in-tree build, bin/urweb under the current
directory or beside this program, run with -boot -noEmacs.  A Nix-packaged
urt has URWEB set to the compiler it was built for.

exit 0
